Z||000000||MooCow||08-15-2000||10:50 PM||||Well I really don't want a stick shift anymore.  There is no way my car is goingto be that much slower if I put in an auto right?  Is it really that difficult to switch from a 4-speed to an auto??  What All must be done?? Websites if any will be fine.<BR>Can I use any auto tranney or only the ones from a Fiero with a v6?<P>                               Thanks Ken<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfixit58||08-16-2000||09:30 AM||||MooCow,<P>There are a couple of topics going in the Tech section about installing an overdrive auto tranny as a replacement for the factory TH125. Those threads would be a good place to start your research. <P>Asside from a trany, you'll need a radiator out of automatic (or a tranny cooler), transmission cooler lines, shifter, shift cable, kick down cable, and auto computer chip. The easiest way would be to find a junker and remove any and all components at one time. You'd probably get your best price as well.<P>Hope this helps,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000002||terryk||08-16-2000||01:18 PM||||I have a little comment to add on the cooler question. Temperature is the main killer of automatics (then RPM's). When I installed the 3800SC and 4T60E in the Ciera GT, I wanted to make sure I had plenty of cooling. I talked with several places and one common point was that I needed to make sure the trans fluid warmed up. Too cold of fluid is bad too. <P>I have a GM aux cooler running into a U-van radiator with dual coolers. The aux basically takes some of the cooling load off the radiator, but the radiator brings the temp of the fluid up quickly.  <P>Consider a Fiero radiator with the built in trans cooler as the primary cooler for the trans. Add a pre-cooler if you want more cooling capacity. I don't recommend using only an external cooler. <P>Terry<BR> ||||reg||1
